   "slug": "score-ranking",
   "title": "Ranking by Total Score",
   "level": 2,
   "statement": "<p>Each student has a Chinese score and a maths score. Sort the students by their total score, highest first. Students with the same total keep the order in which they appear in the input. Print each student with both scores and the total.</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code>. Each of the next <code>n</code> lines holds a name, the Chinese score and the maths score. The fields are separated by one or more spaces.</p>",
   "output": "<p><code>n</code> lines, each <code>name chinese maths total</code> with single spaces, in the sorted order.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n ≤ 100; names are 1 to 10 lowercase letters; scores are between 0 and 100.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "4\ngaoxiang 78 96\nwangxi 70 99\nliujia 90 87\nzhangjin 78 91\n",
     "out": "liujia 90 87 177\ngaoxiang 78 96 174\nwangxi 70 99 169\nzhangjin 78 91 169\n"
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>Use <code>split()</code> with no argument so extra spaces do not matter. A stable sort by the total (biggest first) keeps ties in input order: either sort by <code>-total</code>, or use <code>reverse=True</code>, which is stable too.</p>",
   "solution": "n = int(input())\nrows = []\nfor _ in range(n):\n    name, c, m = input().split()\n    c, m = int(c), int(m)\n    rows.append((name, c, m, c + m))\nrows.sort(key=lambda t: -t[3])\nfor name, c, m, total in rows:\n    print(name, c, m, total)",
