   "slug": "group-anagrams",
   "title": "Anagram Families",
   "level": 2,
   "statement": "<p>Words are in the same <strong>family</strong> if they are anagrams of each other (made of exactly the same letters). Split a list of words into families.</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code>. Each of the next <code>n</code> lines holds one word of lowercase letters.</p>",
   "output": "<p>One line per family: its words in dictionary order, separated by single spaces. A repeated word appears as many times as it was given. The lines are ordered by their first word, in dictionary order.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n ≤ 500; each word has 1 to 8 letters.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "6\nlisten\ngoogle\nenlist\ncat\nsilent\nact\n",
     "out": "act cat\nenlist listen silent\ngoogle\n"
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>Sorting the letters of a word gives a \"family name\" that all its anagrams share, like <code>eilnst</code>. Collect the words in a dictionary keyed by that name, then sort inside and between the families.</p>",
   "solution": "n = int(input())\nfamilies = {}\nfor _ in range(n):\n    w = input()\n    key = ''.join(sorted(w))\n    families.setdefault(key, []).append(w)\ngroups = [sorted(g) for g in families.values()]\ngroups.sort()\nfor g in groups:\n    print(*g)",
