   "title": "Pairs Within Budget",
   "level": 2,
   "statement": "<p>You want to buy exactly two different items, and their prices together must be at most <code>t</code>. How many different pairs of items can you choose? (Two items with the same price are still different items.)</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code> and <code>t</code>. The second line holds the <code>n</code> prices.</p>",
   "output": "<p>One integer: the number of pairs (each pair counted once) whose total is at most <code>t</code>.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n ≤ 1000; 1 ≤ price ≤ 10<sup>6</sup>; 1 ≤ t ≤ 2·10<sup>6</sup>.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "5 8\n5 1 3 7 2\n",
     "out": "7\n"
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>Sort, then put one pointer at the cheapest and one at the most expensive item. If those two fit the budget, then the cheapest item also fits with <em>every</em> item between them. How many is that?</p>",
   "solution": "n, t = map(int, input().split())\na = sorted(map(int, input().split()))\ni, j = 0, n - 1\ncount = 0\nwhile i < j:\n    if a[i] + a[j] <= t:\n        count += j - i\n        i += 1\n    else:\n        j -= 1\nprint(count)",
