   "slug": "kth-of-two-lists",
   "title": "The k-th Number of Two Lists",
   "level": 2,
   "statement": "<p>Two lists are each sorted from smallest to largest. If you put all their numbers together and sorted them, which number would be <code>k</code>-th (counting from 1)?</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code>, <code>m</code> and <code>k</code>. The second line holds <code>n</code> integers in non-decreasing order; the third line holds <code>m</code> integers in non-decreasing order.</p>",
   "output": "<p>One integer: the <code>k</code>-th smallest of all <code>n + m</code> numbers.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n, m ≤ 1000; 1 ≤ k ≤ n + m; all numbers are between −10<sup>6</sup> and 10<sup>6</sup>.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "4 3 5\n1 4 7 8\n2 4 6\n",
     "out": "6\n",
     "why": "<p>All together in order: 1 2 4 4 <b>6</b> 7 8.</p>"
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>Merge with two pointers, but stop as soon as you have taken <code>k</code> numbers.</p>",
   "solution": "n, m, k = map(int, input().split())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\nb = list(map(int, input().split()))\ni = j = 0\nfor _ in range(k):\n    if j == m or (i < n and a[i] <= b[j]):\n        x = a[i]\n        i += 1\n    else:\n        x = b[j]\n        j += 1\nprint(x)",
   "explanation": "",
