   "slug": "version-order",
   "title": "Sorting Version Numbers",
   "level": 2,
   "statement": "<p>Software versions look like <code>1.10.2</code>: whole numbers separated by dots. Version A is older than version B if, comparing the numbers part by part from the left, the first different part is smaller in A. If one version is the start of the other (like <code>1.2</code> and <code>1.2.0</code>), the shorter one is older. Print the versions from oldest to newest.</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code>. Each of the next <code>n</code> lines holds one version.</p>",
   "output": "<p>The versions from oldest to newest, one per line, written exactly as in the input.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n ≤ 500; each version has 1 to 4 parts; each part is a whole number from 0 to 99 written without extra leading zeros. Versions may repeat.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "5\n1.10\n1.2\n1.2.0\n0.9.9\n1.9\n",
     "out": "0.9.9\n1.2\n1.2.0\n1.9\n1.10\n",
     "why": "<p>As text, <code>1.10</code> would come before <code>1.9</code>, but 10 is bigger than 9.</p>"
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>Split a version at the dots and turn the parts into integers. A <em>list</em> or <em>tuple</em> of integers already compares exactly the way this problem wants.</p>",
   "solution": "n = int(input())\nvs = [input().strip() for _ in range(n)]\nvs.sort(key=lambda v: [int(p) for p in v.split('.')])\nfor v in vs:\n    print(v)",
